  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 150 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nBoth ensemble clusters and deterministic guidance show the axis of \nan upper-level ridge centered over the Great Plains. Through Friday, \nthis ridge will gradually shift eastward, but how amplified it \nremains as it does so is uncertain as a shortwave moves into the \nCentral Plains and Upper Midwest. While the NBM shows surface \ntemperatures warming in response to the ridge, a majority of \nguidance has 850mb temperatures holding steady compared to Thursday, \nwith southerly flow east-northeasterly. Climatologically, this \ndoesn't favor warming temperatures, so I wouldn't be surprised to \nsee future NBM runs come in a little cooler. After the effects of \nthe shortwave depart the region on Saturday, ensemble consensus \nshows 850 mb temperatures gradually creeping warmer, favoring a \nsimilar effect at the surface. How warm remains uncertain, as the \nNBM is being driven by bias-corrected guidance, which is some cases \nis nearly 10 degrees warmer than its unbiased counterparts. This \nbias-corrected guidance is training on our recent warmth, so I \nsuspect that it and the resulting NBM are being too aggressive with \nwarming early next week. Going strictly off the 25th and 75th \npercentile of ensemble guidance, the warmest of guidance would give \nus temperatures around 5 degrees above normal, while cooler guidance \nwould be right around climatology. \n\nAs for rain chances, confidence is high that a majority of the area \nwill not experience a soaking rainfall through the weekend. The late-\nweek shortwave will be pivoting northeastward and taking the \ngreatest lift with it. Its trailing cold front will also struggle to \nmake eastward progression into the CWA, limiting rain chances (up to \n50%) to portions of central and northeastern Missouri. This will \nserve as the main focus for rain potential through the forecast \nperiod.",
